      "id": 1,
      "text": "Colver’s name blends Coleman and Weaver, founders of the Ebensburg Coal Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colver%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on the National Register as the Colver Historic District, preserving its coal‑mining heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Colver_Historic_District"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Cambria and Indiana Railroad, headquartered in Colver, once hauled ore and passengers across central Pennsylvania.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/blacklick/colver/colver.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rows of four‑room houses on Reese Avenue were built for immigrant miners who worked the nearby pits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/blacklick/colver/colver.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Colver Company Store, operated until 1963, served as the town’s commercial hub and still stands today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/blacklick/colver/colver.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Colver once boasted a grand hotel that few other coal towns had, a rare luxury for its era.",
